The cyclotron frequency $ f $ of a charged particle in a magnetic field is given by:

$$
f = \frac{qB}{2\pi m}
$$

Solving for mass $ m $:

$$
m = \frac{qB}{2\pi f}
$$

Given:
- $ q = 2e = 2 \times 1.6 \times 10^{-19} = 3.2 \times 10^{-19} $ C
- $ B = \frac{\pi}{4} $ T
- $ f = 1600 $ Hz

Plug in:

$$
m = \frac{(3.2 \times 10^{-19}) \cdot (\pi/4)}{2\pi \cdot 1600}
= \frac{3.2 \times 10^{-19}}{4} \cdot \frac{1}{2 \cdot 1600}
= \frac{3.2 \times 10^{-19}}{4 \cdot 3200}
= \frac{3.2 \times 10^{-19}}{12800}
= 2.5 \times 10^{-23} \text{ kg}
$$

